1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,800
Monday morning, eight fifteen.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,910
You walk up to your regular neighborhood coffee counter to buy a 50,000 VND drink.

3
00:00:05,910 --> 00:00:11,970
Right beside a silent card reader on the wood counter sits a small plastic sign printed with a black-and-white QR square.

4
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:15,830
The cashier points to it with a quick nod: scan the code to transfer, please.

5
00:00:15,830 --> 00:00:19,490
You open your banking app, frame the square, and confirm with biometrics.

6
00:00:19,490 --> 00:00:24,500
Just three seconds later, a small countertop speaker calls out loud and clear: received 50,000 dong.

7
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:30,660
You pick up your coffee and head out, and the shop already holds the full 50,000 VND in its bank account that very morning.

8
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:34,470
Now put that scene right beside the 10,000,000 VND team dinner from Story 1.

9
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:36,770
Both payments happened on a phone at checkout.

10
00:00:36,770 --> 00:00:42,930
Yet tapping a credit card cost the restaurant 220,000 VND in 2.2% fees, leaving only 9,780,000 VND two business days later.

11
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:48,100
Why does one digital tap lose 2.2% and stall for days while a QR scan settles in full in three seconds?

12
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:52,900
The first split comes down to who pulls the trigger on the payment order.

13
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:56,540
When you tap a credit card at checkout, that transaction is a pull payment.

14
00:00:56,540 --> 00:01:04,260
The shop's POS terminal sends a request through the merchant's bank, across the card network, and over to your card issuer asking permission to pull money out.

15
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:07,220
Look at the plastic QR stand on the cafe counter by contrast.

16
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:11,690
It has no battery, no cellular chip, and no power to pull a single dong out of your account.

17
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:15,820
It simply shares two facts with your camera: the shop's bank code and account number.

18
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:22,370
You are the one who opens your banking app, confirms the price, and sends a push payment ordering your bank to move 50,000 VND forward.

19
00:01:22,370 --> 00:01:29,360
Flipping the instruction from pull to push cuts out card terminal authorization fees and four-party card clearing overhead right at the counter.

20
00:01:30,870 --> 00:01:35,170
Even bigger than the direction of the arrow is where the money actually comes from.

21
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:39,150
When you pay with a credit card, your issuing bank is not spending your cash.

22
00:01:39,150 --> 00:01:47,530
It advances its own funds for up to 45 interest-free days, swallows the credit risk if you fail to pay your statement, and peels off a slice for cashback rewards.

23
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:51,770
Those three costs combine into interchange and drive the merchant's 2.2% MDR bill.

24
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:54,930
A QR bank transfer uses a completely different pile of money.

25
00:01:54,930 --> 00:02:03,000
The instant you confirm, your bank subtracts 50,000 VND straight from your existing CASA checking balance—money you already deposited days or weeks earlier.

26
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,450
Nobody floats a 45-day loan.

27
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:05,740
Nobody takes credit risk.

28
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:10,760
Your bank simply debits your deposit ledger and shifts that obligation to the coffee shop's bank.

29
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:14,940
Because no loan ever happened, nobody has an excuse to bill the coffee shop 2.2%.

30
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:20,720
Now slow down those three seconds between tapping confirm and hearing the speaker chime.

31
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:24,210
First, your phone pushes the transfer instruction to your bank.

32
00:02:24,210 --> 00:02:33,730
A moment later, your bank deducts 50,000 VND from your CASA balance and shoots a 24/7 fast-transfer message through the national payment switch straight to the shop's bank.

33
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:40,040
Yet why does the shop's bank dare credit 50,000 VND by second three before any physical cash moves between vaults?

34
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:42,530
The answer is pre-funded clearing collateral.

35
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:48,400
Every bank connected to the 24/7 switch posts liquid reserve pledges or collateral at the clearing center.

36
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:53,220
Backed by that guarantee, the shop's bank treats the electronic message like hard cash.

37
00:02:53,220 --> 00:03:03,170
Under Circular 99/2025/TT-BTC, the shop records Debit Account 112 for demand deposits and Credit Account 511 for sales revenue—full 50,000 VND at T+0, with zero Account 1388 delay.

38
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:09,890
Do banks really wire individual 50,000 VND notes back and forth every time someone orders a latte?

39
00:03:09,890 --> 00:03:10,630
Not a chance.

40
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:16,160
Shipping millions of micro-transfers in real time would gridlock central bank rails before lunch.

41
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:19,010
Instead, modern payment systems run on two clocks.

42
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:26,020
Customer-facing ledgers update in three seconds flat, while interbank cash waits in line for end-of-session net settlement.

43
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:35,310
Suppose over one business day, Bank A customers transfer 100 billion VND across to Bank B, while Bank B customers transfer 96 billion VND back to Bank A (example).

44
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:44,200
When the clearing window shuts, the switch places both totals on a single ledger sheet: 100 billion minus 96 billion leaves a net gap of just 4 billion VND.

45
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:52,830
Bank A settles one 4 billion VND transfer on the Central Bank's reserve ledger, closing 196 billion VND of gross customer activity cleanly in one move.

46
00:03:53,970 --> 00:03:59,980
Let us put the end-of-shift math next to Story 1 so the numbers line up squarely.

47
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:10,070
Imagine the coffee shop sells 200 cups a day at 50,000 VND each—10,000,000 VND in daily revenue, matching our group dinner bill exactly.

48
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:19,720
If every guest paid by credit card at 2.2% MDR, the shop loses 220,000 VND in fees and collects only 9,780,000 VND two days later.

49
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:29,220
When those same 200 customers scan a static QR stand, the shop books 10,000,000 VND straight into Account 112 the very same day.

50
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:43,750
Saving 220,000 VND per day adds up to 6.6 million VND retained each month in our example—enough to cover utility bills or pay an extra part-time barista without raising drink prices a single dong.

51
00:04:44,910 --> 00:04:53,680
If neither you nor the cafe pays a single dong on a static QR transfer, why do commercial banks spend millions running 24/7 servers around the clock?

52
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:56,680
Look at the deposit side of a bank's balance sheet.

53
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:05,680
Before zero-fee QR codes took over street corners, commuters stopped at ATMs every few days and pulled out stacks of paper cash for breakfast and coffee.

54
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:10,090
Paper bills riding in your pocket generate zero deposit funding for a bank.

55
00:05:10,090 --> 00:05:14,740
Today, when you scan a QR code, your paycheck never escapes the banking system.

56
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:21,390
It jumps from your CASA account to the shop's CASA account, and straight onto the milk wholesaler's CASA account.

57
00:05:21,390 --> 00:05:28,980
Banks pay roughly 0.1% a year on those sticky demand deposits while lending them back out to businesses at 8.5% to 10% (example).

58
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:35,110
Giving away free transfers is how banks keep that ultra-cheap funding pool anchored inside their vaults.

59
00:05:36,110 --> 00:05:41,530
Before printing a QR code at home, store owners face one practical design split.

60
00:05:41,530 --> 00:05:47,900
Static QR signs cost zero, yet they do not lock in an order total or link to a receipt number.

61
00:05:47,900 --> 00:06:01,530
During a busy morning rush, a cashier who merely glances at a customer's phone screen without waiting for the speaker chime or live bank statement can easily hand out coffee against a faked screenshot.

62
00:06:01,530 --> 00:06:06,950
Larger chains fix that hole with dynamic QR integrated into their cash register.

63
00:06:06,950 --> 00:06:16,170
Every 50,000 VND cup spawns a one-time QR carrying the exact price and receipt ID, closing the order automatically the moment funds hit.

64
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:31,630
That reconciliation software typically charges around 0.2%—just 20,000 VND on 10,000,000 VND in daily sales, leaving 9,980,000 VND in Account 112 and booking 20,000 VND on Account 641—still 11 times cheaper than credit card MDR.

65
00:06:32,830 --> 00:06:38,670
Every payment rail trades one risk for another, so watch out for three real edge cases.

66
00:06:38,670 --> 00:06:45,510
First, during peak traffic, your banking app may deduct 50,000 VND before the shop's speaker wakes up.

67
00:06:45,510 --> 00:06:57,580
The order is sitting in an interbank clearing queue; shops should wait for a real statement credit rather than giving cash back early in case the switch auto-reverses the transfer.

68
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:04,220
Second, what if a sleepy customer adds an extra zero and wires 500,000 VND for a 50,000 VND coffee?

69
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:16,560
At a local counter, the owner checks the statement, logs the 450,000 VND excess as a temporary payable on Account 338—never as sales revenue—and refunds 450,000 VND back to the sender.

70
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:28,170
Third, remember the buyer's biggest blind spot: QR transfers work like cash out the window, giving you zero credit card Chargeback rights if a remote seller online vanishes.

71
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:34,810
Once you trace the two rails, choosing at the register becomes straightforward.

72
00:07:34,810 --> 00:07:43,050
Standing at a neighborhood cafe with your coffee or groceries right in your hand, scanning QR settles in three seconds.

73
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:51,640
It saves the merchant 220,000 VND on every 10,000,000 VND of sales and keeps your spending grounded in cash you already own.

74
00:07:51,640 --> 00:08:06,110
By contrast, when booking travel months ahead or ordering expensive items online from an unfamiliar store, that 2.2% card fee buys real leverage: a four-party Chargeback dispute path if the goods never arrive.

75
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:10,750
Two tools at the same counter, built for completely different jobs.
